WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.480
Secrets need lifecycle operations after they are created.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.980
Rotation means replacing an old secret with a new value.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.720
Expiration means a secret stops working after a defined time.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.540
Revocation means invalidating a secret immediately,

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:18.920
often during incident response or when access is no longer authorized.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:24.740
These operations are related, but they are not identical, and mature teams understand when each one applies.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.360
Planned rotation is a maintenance activity.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:33.420
A team may rotate database passwords, API keys, certificates, signing keys,

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:37.480
or service account credentials on a schedule or after a role change.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.260
Planned rotation should be documented and tested.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:45.680
The procedure should identify dependencies, update the secret manager, roll applications safely,

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:51.500
verify service health, and remove the old value only after dependent systems are using the new one.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.560
Emergency rotation is different.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:59.220
If exposure is suspected, the first priority is to reduce risk while keeping critical services stable.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:07.200
The safe response is to confirm scope, revoke or rotate the exposed value, update affected services, check for reuse,

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:13.180
review logs for unusual access, notify responsible stakeholders, and document what happened.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:18.920
Do not spend time debating whether the exposed value was probably used before you make it stop working.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:22.340
Dual-secret rollout is a common reliability pattern.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:28.560
A service may temporarily accept both an old and a new secret while clients move to the new value.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:31.900
After verification, the old value is disabled.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.840
This pattern can reduce downtime, but it must be time-bound.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:39.620
If both secrets remain valid indefinitely, the rotation did not reduce risk;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.240
it doubled the number of working credentials.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:46.620
Rotation can break systems when ownership and dependency mapping are weak.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:52.280
A secret might be used by a scheduled job, a backup process, a legacy integration,

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.740
or a manual runbook that is not obvious from the main application repository.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.520
That is why inventory, audit logs, and dependency documentation matter.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:06.140
The better a team understands where a secret is used, the safer rotation becomes.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.560
The best systems make rotation routine rather than frightening.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:16.420
Short-lived credentials, managed identity, automated certificate renewal, versioned secret managers, health checks,

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:19.200
and rollback-aware deployment practices all help.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:21.580
Rotation is not only about compliance.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.560
It is a recovery muscle.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:28.900
A team that can rotate calmly during normal operations is better prepared when a real exposure occurs.
